"Solutions for Advent of Code 2025, Day 06.

Part 1: Problems are arranged in columns — numbers stacked vertically,
operator at the bottom. Extract per-row numbers, transpose to get each
problem's column, apply its operator, and sum the results.

Part 2: Cephalopod math reads right-to-left. Each column holds one
digit per row; read the grid column by column, find where each
problem starts from its operator, read one number per column and
apply the operator found at the bottom of the problem's first column.

Approach: transpose the grid to convert column-oriented layout into
row-oriented data. Part 1 transposes number rows; Part 2 pads the rows
and holds them as vectors of character codes, so it can read any column
by index.

Notable eucalypt techniques:
- `transpose` for matrix pivoting (pipeline form `padded transpose`)
- `zip-with` to pair operators with number lists
- `zip-with` over problem start and end columns
- `product` and `sum` as the two operators
- `foldl-strict` with expression anaphora `_0 * 10 + _1`
- `str.format` padding, `str.code-points` and `vec.of` for O(1) column reads

Running time: well under 1s (both parts)
"

` "Extract all numbers from a line, splitting on whitespace."
line-nums(line): line str.split-on(" +") filter(!= "") map(num)

` "Extract operators from the operator line."
ops(line): line str.letters filter(!= " ")

` "Apply an operator to a list of numbers."
apply-op(op, nums): (op = "*") then(product(nums), sum(nums))

solve(data): {
  lines: data filter(!= "")
  num-lines: lines butlast
  op-line: lines last
  problems: num-lines map(line-nums) transpose
}.(zip-with(apply-op, ops(op-line), problems) sum)

` "The code point of a space, as found in the grid."
space: ch.code-point(" ")

` "Convert a list of digit values to a number: [3,6,9] -> 369."
digits-to-num(ds): ds foldl-strict(_0 * 10 + _1, 0)

` "Each row is padded to the grid width `w` and held as a vector of
character codes, so that a column is read with O(1) indexing rather than
by transposing lists of characters."
code-row(w, line): line str.format("%-{w}s") str.code-points vec.of

` "Part 2 reads one number per column: the digits down the number rows,
as character codes (`0` is 48) with the spaces skipped."
column-num(num-rows, j): num-rows map(vec.nth(j)) filter(!= space) map(- 48) digits-to-num

solve2(data): {
  lines: data filter(!= "")
  w: lines map(str.len) max-of
  rows: lines map(code-row(w))
  num-rows: rows butlast
  op-row: rows last
  op-column?(j): vec.nth(j, op-row) != space
  ` "A problem starts at each column holding an operator and runs up to
the all-space column before the next one."
  starts: range(0, w) filter(op-column?)
  ends: ((starts tail) ++ [w + 1]) map(dec)
  solve-problem(s, e): apply-op(ch.of-code-point(op-row vec.nth(s)), range(s, e) map(column-num(num-rows)))
}.(zip-with(solve-problem, starts, ends) sum)

` { target: :test
    import: "ex1=inputs/day06-ex1.txt" }
test: {
  part-1: solve(ex1) //= 4277556
  part-2: solve2(ex1) //= 3263827
  RESULT: (part-1 ∧ part-2) then(:PASS, :FAIL)
}
